# Debt Settlement Agent

Voice agent that negotiates debt settlements. Code-based policy decides every move; the LLM only extracts terms (NLU) and phrases moves with fact placeholders (NLG). The vendored `feasibility/` engine is the source of truth for every number.

## Before doing anything in a new chat
1. Read `docs/PROGRESS.md` (phase status, interfaces built so far, deviations, open issues).
2. Read only the `docs/PLAN.md` sections your phase prompt names. Do not read the whole plan unless needed.
3. Read existing code only when you need an interface not recorded in `docs/PROGRESS.md`.

## Ground rules
- Money is integer cents. Settlement percentages are integer basis points (4500 = 45%). Convert to the engine with `Decimal(bp) / Decimal(10000)`. Never use float for money. Dates are `datetime.date`.
- `feasibility/` is read-only except for bug fixes, and every fix ships with a test.
- The LLM never produces digits or number words for speech. Spoken numbers only come from `Fact.render()`.
- The NLG prompt never receives PRIVATE facts or client financials.
- All LLM and STT calls go through `app/llm/client.py`, by role (`nlu`, `nlg`, `sim`, `stt`), never by model name.
- `sim/` must not import `app.agent`.
- Every belief change, block, escalation, and LLM call is appended to the audit log.
- Python 3.12, FastAPI, Pydantic v2, pytest, ruff. Type hints on all public functions.
- Synthetic data only. Do not copy third-party assignment text or its `cases/`.
- Add a dependency only with a one-line justification in the commit message.
- Stay inside your phase's scope. Do not start the next phase.

## Module and comment style
- Every new or touched `.py` module under `app/`, `sim/`, and `eval/` must open with a **module docstring** that a stranger can skim: what the file is for, what it does *not* do when that is easy to confuse (e.g. `numbers.py` vs `units.py`), and who calls it or where it sits in the pipeline.
- Prefer a short multi-line opener (about 4–10 lines) over a one-liner title.
- Public classes and functions get a one-line docstring stating the contract; expand only when the reason codes, units, or failure modes are non-obvious.
- Do not narrate obvious code line-by-line. Comments explain *why*, invariants, and order-sensitive logic (guard reason codes, extract priority, money↔count cross-match).

## End of every phase
1. Run the phase's acceptance check, plus `pytest -q` and `ruff check .`. All green.
2. Update `docs/PROGRESS.md`: mark the phase done, list the files added, record the public interfaces (signatures) later phases need, and record any deviation from PLAN.md and open issues.
3. Commit with message `phase N: <summary>`.
